{"id": 3, "ts": 1792529247.4175148, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_3", "message": "I\u2019d like to open the discussion by putting forward Candidate\u202fB as my provisional recommendation.\u202fFrom the evidence we have, B is very conscientious, which speaks directly to operational reliability, and handles stress very well\u2014both critical for long\u2011distance flights.\u202fTheir ability to assess weather conditions and strong computer skills also demonstrate solid technical and cognitive competence for complex, international operations.\nThe only noted drawback is that B is sometimes perceived as pretentious and may adopt the wrong tone when communicating. While this raises a crew\u2011cooperation flag, it is a behavioral issue that can be addressed through targeted training and feedback, and it does not outweigh the core safety\u2011critical strengths B brings.\nIn contrast, Candidate\u202fA, despite strong anticipation and leadership, is described as unfriendly and has unhealthy habits that could affect reliability and crew dynamics. Candidate\u202fC makes quick correct decisions and shows excellent attention, but struggles with communication and is seen as egocentric, which may be harder to remediate. Candidate\u202fD excels in problem solving and responsibility but is considered moody and a loner, raising concerns about consistent crew cooperation on long hauls.\nGiven the balance of strengths and manageable weaknesses, I believe Candidate\u202fB currently offers the strongest overall fit for the long\u2011distance pilot role.", "thoughts": "We need to start the discussion. No prior contributions. As first panel member, we need to make a focused argumentative contribution, stating our current recommendation, referencing discussion (none), using explicit candidate facts, and explain implication.\n\nWe have candidate facts. Need to evaluate based on criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, calmness, c